    "media:imagetext": "- 3-\nDoc. 27th. En route south from Thursday Island. We arrived at Thured y which\nis about twice as big as blay Island or actually two miles around. Debby's friend Reg\nboott met us and escorted us to the Grand Hotel on a slight rise about the harbor.\nur room was on the second floor reached from an open porch.\nIt had a large door\nopening on to the porch but no other ventilation whatever and it was hot. Thursday\nIsland is just a little more than 10 degrees below the equator. Reg told us that\nthe coldest day last winter the temperature dropped to 68 degrees at 2 A.M.\nGrand boasts of \"Septic accomodations\" which means you con't have to use a\n\"Chic\ndale\". However, the toilet for men, such as it was, was at the end of the long\nwooden porch and the ladies around the corner from there.\nWe left these sumptious\nsurroundings at once and went to keg's house by taxi. It is on a hill side over\nlooking the harbor. We met Mr.s Scott, very cordial and nice and their little girl\nage 5, quite cute. Mrs. Scott busied herself with getting dinner while Debby and\nReg started looking over drawer after drawer of fine shells, many quite rare. He\nlet her buy anything she wanted except two or three he wanted to keep for one\nreason or another.\nDinner was announced at 7 P.M. and we had perfectly delicious\napparently with a large deep freeze filled with venison, wild pig and mackerel the •\nscott family pretty much lives off the tani, after dinner Reg andy Debby looked st\nshells until 11 P.M., agreed on a selection, got together on cost and he agreed to\npsor and ship to Captiva.\noR more hospitable. He walked down to the hotel with us and we said our au revoirs.\nIt was \"Caberet\" night at the Grand. Alot of pretty rough looking customers, black,\nwhite and brown were dringing beer and dancing to the music of a three man orchestra\nwhose playing xxx made up in sound for what it lacked in quality. In our room on\nthe second floor balcony we haddall the privacy of the proverbial gold fish for we\ncouldn't close our door without suffocating. However, the music or should I say\nnoise, abated around midnight and we slept until called at 6 a.m. to catch the\nplane on which I am writing now. So Thursday Island has come and gone. It was two xa\nye irs ago that Debby got a letter from Keg scott in which he said that he wanted\nto sell his collection. I said \"Why don't you buy it ?\" and Deb said \"I wouldn't\nwant to buy any part of it without seeing it\" so I said \"let's go look at it \". \"We\ncan't do that\" said Deb and I said \"Why not?\" And here we are, mission secomplished.\nDebby has some wonderful and very rare shells to add to her collection and is\nquite happy.\nComing south we landed at Weipa on the York Peninsula. It is the\ncenter of an almost unlimited supply of bauxite, the ore used to produce aluminum.\nFurther down the coast there are large coal deposits. They hope to combine the two\nto produce aluminum econnmically. Our next stop is Cooktown and then Cairns\nwhere wo he ve a four hour stopover before taking a larger and faster Viscount\nto Rockhampton.\nI'll get this off from Cairns as it is already too long. We'll probably\nstLy several days in the Rockhampton area going out to the Great Barrier Reef and\nseeing a number of shell dealers who have been highly recommended.\nThen to\nSydney and across Australia to Pérth. We haven't decided whether we'll go by air,\ntrain or bus. The latter would give us the best look at the country but four days\nof that travel might be a little rough for us old folks. Anyway, our addrees until\nny next letter will be care of PAA, 82 Blizabeth St., Sydney.\nMay '64 bring you more - of everything you want.\nHarold\nSI SHISHPIOS",
